etrics</source><volume>62</volume><fpage>526</fpage><lpage>33</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16918917</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b35-cin-6-0369"><label>35</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Qin</surname><given-names>LX</given-names></name><name><surname>Self</surname><given-names>SG</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>On comparing the clustering of regression models method with K-means clustering. Working Paper. Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center</article-title><source>Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics</source></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b36-cin-6-0369"><label>36</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lagos-Quintana</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>New microRNAs from mouse and human</article-title><source>RNA</source><volume>9</volume><fpage>175</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12554859</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b37-cin-6-0369"><label>37</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Farh</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>The widespread impact of mammalian MicroRNAs on mRNA repression and evolution</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>310</volume><fpage>1817</fpage><lpage>21</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16308420</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b38-cin-6-0369"><label>38</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brennecke</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Principles of microRNA&#x02013;target recognition</article-title><source>PLoS Biol</source><volume>3</volume><fpage>e85</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15723116</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b39-cin-6-0369"><label>39</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Enright</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>MicroRNA target detection</article-title><source>Genome Biology</source><volume>5</volume><fpage>R.1</fpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b40-cin-6-0369"><label>40</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krek</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Combinatorial microRNA target predictions</article-title><source>Nature Genetics</source><volume>37</volume><fpage>495</fpage><lpage>500</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15806104</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b41-cin-6-0369"><label>41</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Prediction of mammalian microRNA targets</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>115</volume><fpage>787</fpage><lpage>98</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14697198</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b42-cin-6-0369"><label>42</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sethupathy</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>A guide through present computational approaches for the identification of mammalian microRNA targets</article-title><source>Nature Methods</source><volume>3</volume><fpage>881</fpage><lpage>6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17060911</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b43-cin-6-0369"><label>43</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sethupathy</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>TarBase: A comprehensive database of experimentally supported animal microRNA targets</article-title><source>RNA</source><volume>12</volume><fpage>192</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16373484</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b44-cin-6-0369"><label>44</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Storey</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Tibshirani</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Statistical significance for genome-wide studies</article-title><source>PNAS</source><volume>100</volume><fpage>9440</fpage><lpage>5</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12883005</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b45-cin-6-0369"><label>45</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>RAS is regulated by the let-7 microRNA family</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>120</volume><fpage>635</fpage><lpage>647</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15766527</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b46-cin-6-0369"><label>46</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Croce</surname><given-names>CM</given-names></name><name><surname>Calin</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>miRNAs, cancer, and stem cell division</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>122</volume><fpage>6</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16009126</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Figure 1a. Sample clustering based on miRNA expression. The heatmap represents the co-clustering frequency between samples: the co-clustering frequency on the diagonal is always 1, as a sample is always clustered with itself. The dendrogram represents the sample clustering using the hierarchical clustering method with the co-clustering frequency as the similarity measure. Each sample is denoted with its disease status (T = Tumor, N = Normal) and tissue type (BRST = Breast, COLON, KID = Kidney, LUNG, PROST = Prostate).</p><p>Figure 1b. Sample clustering based on gene expression.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f1a"/><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f1b"/></fig><fig id="f2-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Boxplot for the 38 cancer-related miRNAs among normal samples (grey) and tumor samples (red).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Figure 3a. (left panel)&#x02014;Hierarchical clustering of the 38 cancer-related miRNAs based on the miRNA-gene correlations among tumor samples. Heatmap represents the miRNA-genes correlations, with columns for miRNAs and rows for genes.</p><p>Figure 3b. (right panel)&#x02014;Hierarchical clustering of the 38 cancer-related miRNAs based on the miRNA-gene correlations among normal samples.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><title>Profile plot for CORM clusters of the 38 cancer-related miRNAs</title><p>Each panel is for a miRNA cluster and each line is for a miRNA. X axis represents combinations of disease status (T = Tumor, N = Normal) and tissue type (BRST=Breast, COLON, KID = Kidney, LUNG, PROST = Prostate). Y axis is the mean miRNA expression level for a given tissue type and disease status.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f4"/></fig><fig id="f5-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Figure 5a. Scatter plot for RASSF7 expression <italic>versus</italic> let-7f expression. X axis is let-7f expression and Y axis is RASSF7 expression. Tissue type is labeled under each point. Color of the label represents disease status: red for tumor and blue for normal.</p><p>Figure 5b. Scatter plot for RAB34 expression <italic>versus</italic> let-7f expression.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f5"/></fig><fig id="f6-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><title>Profile plot for CORM clusters of the 178 genes predicted as let-7f targets</title><p>Genes were clustered based on their relationship with let-7f and disease status using the CORM method. Each panel is for a gene cluster and each line is for a gene. X axis represents four conditions: normal, normal with one unit let-7f expression, tumor, and tumor with one unit let-7f expression. Y axis represents the average gene expression level for a given condition.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cin-6-0369f6"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-cin-6-0369" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>The 38 cancer-related miRNAs with p-value &#x0003c; 0.001 and fold-change &#x0003e;3.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cluster</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Name</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fold-change</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P-value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">hsa-miR-23b</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.11</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.37E-09</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">hsa-miR-26b_(sub_1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.34</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.53E-10</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">hsa-miR-125b</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspa